<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · movement</p><h1 class="enc-title">Northumbrian Renaissance</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">late 7th–8th century</span> · <a href="/centuries/7th-century">7th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Northumbrian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/renaissance">Renaissance</a> was the flowering of learning and art in the monasteries of Northumbria in the late seventh and eighth centuries, at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lindisfarne">Lindisfarne</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/monkwearmouth-jarrow-abbey">Wearmouth–Jarrow</a>, Whitby, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hexham">Hexham</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/york">York</a>. Blending Irish, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anglo-saxons">Anglo-Saxon</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a> traditions, it produced the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lindisfarne-gospels">Lindisfarne Gospels</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/codex-amiatinus">Codex Amiatinus</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ruthwell-cross">Ruthwell Cross</a> and the scholarship of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bede">Bede</a>, and through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alcuin-of-york">Alcuin</a> it influenced the Carolingian revival.</p></div>
